          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes etcd must use TLS to protect the confidentiality of sensitive data during electronic dissemination. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes etcd must use TLS to protect the confidentiality of sensitive data during electronic dissemination. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242381",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242381",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000023-CTR-000055",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242381",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000023-CTR-000055",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242381r1043176_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242381r1043176",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-000220",
          "rule_title": "The Kubernetes Controller Manager must create unique service accounts for each work payload.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes Controller Manager manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane.\n\nSet the value of \"--use-service-account-credentials\" to \"true\".",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\n\ngrep -i use-service-account-credentials * \n\nIf the setting \"--use-service-account-credentials\" is not configured in the Kubernetes Controller Manager manifest file or it is set to \"false\", this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The Kubernetes Controller Manager is a background process that embeds core control loops regulating cluster system state through the API Server. Every process executed in a pod has an associated service account. By default, service accounts use the same credentials for authentication. Implementing the default settings poses a High risk to the Kubernetes Controller Manager. Setting the \"--use-service-account-credential\" value lowers the attack surface by generating unique service accounts settings for each controller instance.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000015"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "52bfd175-13a0-5403-852e-6fac01e04187",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The exact official check is controller-manager --use-service-account-credentials=true, not simply the existence of application service accounts. Retain the proposed MPN host inheritance designation, with application named service accounts as separate supporting workload isolation evidence; do not claim the chart proves this controller flag.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Link the controller-manager setting from the MPN baseline; retain workload account manifests separately. No Horizon account-management implementation change follows from this host-owned rule.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-workload-sa] re-kubernetes-workload-sa\nApplication chart creates named service accounts and explicitly controls automountServiceAccountToken. That supports workload isolation but does not establish the separate controller-manager --use-service-account-credentials flag.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/serviceaccount.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The exact official check is controller-manager --use-service-account-credentials=true, not simply the existence of application service accounts. Retain the proposed MPN host inheritance designation, with application named service accounts as separate supporting workload isolation evidence; do not claim the chart proves this controller flag.\n\nNext action: Link the controller-manager setting from the MPN baseline; retain workload account manifests separately. No Horizon account-management implementation change follows from this host-owned rule."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242382",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242382",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000090",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242382",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000090",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242382r1137638_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242382r1137638",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-000270",
          "rule_title": "The Kubernetes API Server must enable Node,RBAC as the authorization mode.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes API Server manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. \n\nSet the value of \"--authorization-mode\" to \"Node,RBAC\".",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\ngrep -i authorization-mode * \n\nIf the setting authorization-mode is set to \"AlwaysAllow\" in the Kubernetes API Server manifest file or is not configured, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To mitigate the risk of unauthorized access to sensitive information by entities that have been issued certificates by DOD-approved PKIs, all DOD systems (e.g., networks, web servers, and web portals) must be properly configured to incorporate access control methods that do not rely solely on the possession of a certificate for access. Successful authentication must not automatically give an entity access to an asset or security boundary. Authorization procedures and controls must be implemented to ensure each authenticated entity also has a validated and current authorization. Authorization is the process of determining whether an entity, once authenticated, is permitted to access a specific asset.\n\nNode,RBAC is the method within Kubernetes to control access of users and applications. Kubernetes uses roles to grant authorization API requests made by kubelets.\n\nSatisfies: SRG-APP-000340-CTR-000770, SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000095, SRG-APP-000378-CTR-000880, SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000090",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000213"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "c7a66d8e-aa7f-5d88-ad26-366fd30967d5",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes API Server must enable Node,RBAC as the authorization mode. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes API Server must enable Node,RBAC as the authorization mode. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242383",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242383",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000038-CTR-000105",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242383",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000038-CTR-000105",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242383r1137641_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242383r1137641",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-000290",
          "rule_title": "User-managed resources must be created in dedicated namespaces.",
          "fix_text": "Move any user-managed resources from the default, kube-public, and kube-node-lease namespaces to user namespaces.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "To view the available namespaces, run the command:\n\nkubectl get namespaces\n\nThe default namespaces to be validated are default, kube-public, and kube-node-lease if it is created.\n\nFor the default namespace, execute the commands:\n\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=default\nkubectl get all\n\nFor the kube-public namespace, execute the commands:\n\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=kube-public\nkubectl get all\n\nFor the kube-node-lease namespace, execute the commands:\n\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=kube-node-lease\nkubectl get all\n\nThe only valid return values are the kubernetes service (i.e., service/kubernetes) and nothing at all.\n\nIf a return value is returned from the \"kubectl get all\" command and it is not the kubernetes service (i.e., service/kubernetes), this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Creating namespaces for user-managed resources is important when implementing Role-Based Access Controls (RBAC). RBAC allows for the authorization of users and helps support proper API server permissions separation and network micro segmentation. If user-managed resources are placed within the default namespaces, it becomes impossible to implement policies for RBAC permission, service account usage, network policies, and more.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "c6e52086-6b72-5ae1-9112-cd05e5962489",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_a_finding",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Compliant\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: All chart declarations use the dedicated horizon-uds namespace, backed by its namespace manifest. Source/configuration recommendation; no live namespace inventory is claimed.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "All chart declarations use the dedicated horizon-uds namespace, backed by its namespace manifest. Source/configuration recommendation; no live namespace inventory is claimed."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242384",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242384",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000090",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242384",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000090",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242384r1137638_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242384r1137638",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-000300",
          "rule_title": "The Kubernetes Scheduler must have secure binding.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes Scheduler manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Set the argument \"--bind-address\" to \"127.0.0.1\".",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\n\ngrep -i bind-address * \n\nIf the setting \"bind-address\" is not set to \"127.0.0.1\" or is not found in the Kubernetes Scheduler manifest file, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Limiting the number of attack vectors and implementing authentication and encryption on the endpoints available to external sources is paramount when securing the overall Kubernetes cluster. The Scheduler API service exposes port 10251/TCP by default for health and metrics information use. This port does not encrypt or authenticate connections. If this port is exposed externally, an attacker can use this port to attack the entire Kubernetes cluster. By setting the bind address to localhost (i.e., 127.0.0.1), only those internal services that require health and metrics information can access the Scheduler API.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000213"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "3e2480a2-d694-5133-8062-64ff0c0fd851",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes Scheduler must have secure binding. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes Scheduler must have secure binding. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
