          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes Controllers must enforce ports, protocols, and services (PPS) that adhere to the Ports, Protocols, and Services Management Category Assurance List (PPSM CAL). The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes Controllers must enforce ports, protocols, and services (PPS) that adhere to the Ports, Protocols, and Services Management Category Assurance List (PPSM CAL). The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242413",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242413",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142-CTR-000325",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242413",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142-CTR-000325",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242413r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242413r1043177",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-000950",
          "rule_title": "The Kubernetes etcd must enforce ports, protocols, and services (PPS) that adhere to the Ports, Protocols, and Services Management Category Assurance List (PPSM CAL).",
          "fix_text": "Amend any system documentation requiring revision. Update Kubernetes etcd manifest and namespace PPS configuration to comply with PPSM CAL.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\ngrep kube-apiserver.manifest -I -etcd-servers *\n-edit etcd-main.manifest file:\nVIM <Manifest Name:\nReview  livenessProbe:\nHttpGet:\nPort:\nReview ports:\n- containerPort:\n       hostPort:\n- containerPort:\n       hostPort:\nRun Command:\nkubectl describe services –all-namespace\nSearch labels for any apiserver names spaces.\nPort:\n\nAny manifest and namespace PPS configuration not in compliance with PPSM CAL is a finding.\n\nReview the information systems documentation and interview the team, gain an understanding of the etcd architecture, and determine applicable PPS. Any PPS in the system documentation not in compliance with the CAL PPSM is a finding. Any PPS not set in the system documentation is a finding.\n\nReview findings against the most recent PPSM CAL:\nhttps://cyber.mil/ppsm/cal/\n\nVerify etcd network boundary with the PPS associated with the CAL Assurance Categories. Any PPS not in compliance with the CAL Assurance Category requirements is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Kubernetes etcd PPS must be controlled and conform to the PPSM CAL. Those PPS that fall outside the PPSM CAL must be blocked. Instructions on the PPSM can be found in DoD Instruction 8551.01 Policy.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "216d3e4f-a802-54bb-882c-328df6e446ff",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes etcd must enforce ports, protocols, and services (PPS) that adhere to the Ports, Protocols, and Services Management Category Assurance List (PPSM CAL). The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes etcd must enforce ports, protocols, and services (PPS) that adhere to the Ports, Protocols, and Services Management Category Assurance List (PPSM CAL). The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242414",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242414",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142-CTR-000330",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242414",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142-CTR-000330",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242414r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242414r1043177",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-000960",
          "rule_title": "The Kubernetes cluster must use non-privileged host ports for user pods.",
          "fix_text": "For any of the pods that are using host-privileged ports, reconfigure the pod to use a service to map a host non-privileged port to the pod port or reconfigure the image to use non-privileged ports.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "On the Control Plane, run the command:\nkubectl get pods --all-namespaces\n\nThe list returned is all pods running within the Kubernetes cluster. For those pods running within the user namespaces (System namespaces are kube-system, kube-node-lease and kube-public), run the command:\nkubectl get pod podname -o yaml | grep -i port\n\nNote: In the above command, \"podname\" is the name of the pod. For the command to work correctly, the current context must be changed to the namespace for the pod. The command to do this is:\n\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=namespace-name\n(Note: \"namespace-name\" is the name of the namespace.)\n\nReview the ports that are returned for the pod.\n\nIf any host-privileged ports are returned for any of the pods, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Privileged ports are those ports below 1024 and that require system privileges for their use. If containers can use these ports, the container must be run as a privileged user. Kubernetes must stop containers that try to map to these ports directly. Allowing non-privileged ports to be mapped to the container-privileged port is the allowable method when a certain port is needed. An example is mapping port 8080 externally to port 80 in the container.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "9638a2f6-cfa3-5001-85bd-e4c7aadf3c72",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Candidate workloads expose non-host application ports; UDS RestrictHostPorts/DisallowNodePort policy rejects unapproved host-port exposure unless explicitly exempted.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[app-pods] app-pods\nCandidate service chart applies non-root/read-only/no-escalation contexts, Secret references, probes and resource settings. Rendered app pods corroborate those settings; some init containers lack explicit resources.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Candidate workloads expose non-host application ports; UDS RestrictHostPorts/DisallowNodePort policy rejects unapproved host-port exposure unless explicitly exempted.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242415",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242415",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000171-CTR-000435",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242415",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000171-CTR-000435",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242415r1069466_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242415r1069466",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001160",
          "rule_title": "Secrets in Kubernetes must not be stored as environment variables.",
          "fix_text": "Any secrets stored as environment variables must be moved to the secret files with the proper protections and enforcements or placed within a password vault.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Follow these steps to check, from the Kubernetes control plane, if secrets are stored as environment variables.\n\n1. Find All Pods Using Secrets in Environment Variables.\n\nTo list all pods using secrets as environment variables, execute:\n\nkubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o yaml | grep -A5 \"secretKeyRef\"\n\nIf any of the values returned reference environment variables, this is a finding.\n\n2. Check Environment Variables in a Specific Pod.\n\nTo check if a specific pod is using secrets as environment variables, execute:\n\nkubectl get pods -n <namespace>\n(Replace <namespace> with the actual namespace, or omit -n <namespace> to check in the default namespace.)\nkubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> | grep -A5 \"Environment:\"\n\nIf secrets are used, output like the following will be displayed:\n\nEnvironment:\n  SECRET_USERNAME:   <set from secret: my-secret key: username>\n  SECRET_PASSWORD:   <set from secret: my-secret key: password>\n\nIf the output is similar to this, the pod is using Kubernetes secrets as environment variables, and this is a finding.\n\n3. Check the Pod YAML for Secret Usage.\n\nTo check the full YAML definition for environment variables, execute:\n\nkubectl get pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml | grep -A5 \"env:\"\n\nExample output:\nyaml\nCopyEdit\nenv:\n  - name: SECRET_USERNAME\n    valueFrom:\n      secretKeyRef:\n        name: my-secret\n        key: username\n\nThis means the pod is pulling the secret named my-secret and setting SECRET_USERNAME from its username key.\n\nIf the pod is pulling a secret and setting an environment variable in the \"env:\", this is a finding.\n\n4. Check Secrets in a Deployment, StatefulSet, or DaemonSet.\n\nIf the pod is managed by a Deployment, StatefulSet, or DaemonSet, check their configurations:\n\nkubectl get deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml | grep -A5 \"env:\"\n\nor\n\nFor all Deployments in all namespaces:\n\nkubectl get deployments --all-namespaces -o yaml | grep -A5 \"env:\"\n\nIf the pod is pulling a secret and setting an environment variable in the \"env:\", this is a finding.\n\n5. Check Environment Variables Inside a Running Pod.\n\nIf needed, check the environment variables inside a running pod:\n\nkubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- env | grep SECRET\n\nIf any of the values returned reference environment variables, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Secrets, such as passwords, keys, tokens, and certificates must not be stored as environment variables. These environment variables are accessible inside Kubernetes by the \"Get Pod\" API call, and by any system, such as CI/CD pipeline, which has access to the definition file of the container. Secrets must be mounted from files or stored within password vaults.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004062",
            "CCI-000196"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "bc88ff5a-1325-5c80-9a2b-bdc7e385fa54",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual pushed UDS profile still renders POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD as env.valueFrom.secretKeyRef. The official requirement is specifically about secret delivery: using a Kubernetes Secret as the source does not satisfy its prohibition on environment variables. Pepr admission, encrypted mesh and RBAC protect other attack paths but do not remove secret values from the process environment. Use protected credential delivery or document an accepted exception. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work and noted a possible conflict between different customers' credential-delivery requirements; that conflict is recalled but not yet verified. Confirm the applicable MPN/customer requirements before changing shared defaults. Use an appropriate deployment profile where requirements differ, and retain any approved exception rather than assuming one universal delivery method.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm current secret delivery and retrieve applicable MPN and other customer credential-delivery requirements to verify the reported possible conflict. Check consumer support for protected file/vault delivery and define customer-specific profiles if necessary. For the assessed MPN scope, migrate affected secret-as-environment consumers while preserving authentication, or retain a formally accepted exception with its rationale. Do not change shared defaults for every customer without resolving the conflicting requirements. Retain requirement references, profile scope and verification evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-postgres-env] re-kubernetes-postgres-env\nPOSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_PASSWORD are injected with env.valueFrom.secretKeyRef. Re-rendering after actual release apply_identity/stage transformations preserves both environment references; supported mesh or Secret RBAC does not change this delivery mechanism.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-kubernetes-profile-transform] re-kubernetes-profile-transform\nUDS identity transformation removes the bundled Keycloak component/secrets and edits OIDC/profile values, but does not replace the PostgreSQL secret-as-environment template or SeaweedFS credential ConfigMap. runtime_source.stage pins image fields and leaves those templates unchanged.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/platform_identity.py",
          "finding_details": "Actual pushed UDS profile still renders POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD as env.valueFrom.secretKeyRef. The official requirement is specifically about secret delivery: using a Kubernetes Secret as the source does not satisfy its prohibition on environment variables. Pepr admission, encrypted mesh and RBAC protect other attack paths but do not remove secret values from the process environment. Use protected credential delivery or document an accepted exception. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work and noted a possible conflict between different customers' credential-delivery requirements; that conflict is recalled but not yet verified. Confirm the applicable MPN/customer requirements before changing shared defaults. Use an appropriate deployment profile where requirements differ, and retain any approved exception rather than assuming one universal delivery method.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm current secret delivery and retrieve applicable MPN and other customer credential-delivery requirements to verify the reported possible conflict. Check consumer support for protected file/vault delivery and define customer-specific profiles if necessary. For the assessed MPN scope, migrate affected secret-as-environment consumers while preserving authentication, or retain a formally accepted exception with its rationale. Do not change shared defaults for every customer without resolving the conflicting requirements. Retain requirement references, profile scope and verification evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242417",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242417",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000211-CTR-000530",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242417",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000211-CTR-000530",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242417r1137643_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242417r1137643",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001360",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes must separate user functionality.",
          "fix_text": "Move any user pods that are present in the Kubernetes system namespaces to user specific namespaces.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "On the Control Plane, run the command:\nkubectl get pods --all-namespaces\n\nReview the namespaces and pods that are returned. Kubernetes system namespaces are kube-node-lease, kube-public, and kube-system.\n\nIf any user pods are present in the Kubernetes system namespaces, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Separating user functionality from management functionality is a requirement for all the components within the Kubernetes Control Plane. Without the separation, users may have access to management functions that can degrade the Kubernetes architecture and the services being offered, and can offer a method to bypass testing and validation of functions before introduced into a production environment.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001082"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "5d79cdc5-f062-5822-a0b5-3a867a77aaf8",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Separate tenant/admin gateways, namespace RBAC and non-root admission controls separate user workload functions from platform administration.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Separate tenant/admin gateways, namespace RBAC and non-root admission controls separate user workload functions from platform administration.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242418",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242418",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242418",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242418r1043178_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242418r1043178",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001400",
          "rule_title": "The Kubernetes API server must use approved cipher suites.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes API Server manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. \n\nSet the value of \"--tls-cipher-suites\" to:\n\"TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384,TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\"",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\ngrep -i tls-cipher-suites *\n\nIf the setting feature tls-cipher-suites is not set in the Kubernetes API server manifest file or contains no value or does not contain TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384,TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The Kubernetes API server communicates to the kubelet service on the nodes to deploy, update, and delete resources. If an attacker were able to get between this communication and modify the request, the Kubernetes cluster could be compromised. Using approved cypher suites for the communication ensures the protection of the transmitted information, confidentiality, and integrity so that the attacker cannot read or alter this communication.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001184"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "54e440f9-4deb-58a0-b9d1-080277da122c",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes API server must use approved cipher suites. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: The Kubernetes API server must use approved cipher suites. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242419",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242419",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242419",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242419r1043178_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242419r1043178",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001410",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes API Server must have the SSL Certificate Authority set.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes API Server manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. \n\nSet the value of \"--client-ca-file\" to path containing Approved Organizational Certificate.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\ngrep -i client-ca-file *\n\nIf the setting feature client-ca-file is not set in the Kubernetes API server manifest file or contains no value, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Kubernetes control plane and external communication are managed by API Server. The main implementation of the API Server is to manage hardware resources for pods and containers using horizontal or vertical scaling. Anyone who can access the API Server can effectively control the Kubernetes architecture. Using authenticity protection, the communication can be protected against man-in-the-middle attacks/session hijacking and the insertion of false information into sessions.\n\nThe communication session is protected by utilizing transport encryption protocols such as TLS. TLS provides the Kubernetes API Server with a means to authenticate sessions and encrypt traffic. \n\nTo enable encrypted communication for API Server, the parameter client-ca-file must be set. This parameter gives the location of the SSL Certificate Authority file used to secure API Server communication.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001184"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "58e0acf3-2aa4-5a8f-a0f6-e2ae8846cd74",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: Kubernetes API Server must have the SSL Certificate Authority set. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: Kubernetes API Server must have the SSL Certificate Authority set. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-242420",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-242420",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-242420",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-242420r1043178_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-242420r1043178",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001420",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes Kubelet must have the SSL Certificate Authority set.",
          "fix_text": "On the Control Plane, run the command:\nps -ef | grep kubelet\n\nRemove the \"--client-ca-file\" option if present.\n\nNote the path to the config file (identified by --config).\n\nEdit the Kubernetes Kubelet config file: \nSet the value of \"clientCAFile\" to a path containing an Approved Organizational Certificate. \n\nRestart the kubelet service using the following command:\nsystemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart kubelet",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "On the Control Plane, run the command:\nps -ef | grep kubelet\n\nIf the \"--client-ca-file\" option exists, this is a finding.\n\nNote the path to the config file (identified by --config).\n\nRun the command:\ngrep -i clientCAFile <path_to_config_file>\n\nIf the setting \"clientCAFile\" is not set or contains no value, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Kubernetes container and pod configuration are maintained by Kubelet. Kubelet agents register nodes with the API Server, mount volume storage, and perform health checks for containers and pods. Anyone who gains access to Kubelet agents can effectively control applications within the pods and containers. Using authenticity protection, the communication can be protected against man-in-the-middle attacks/session hijacking and the insertion of false information into sessions.\n\nThe communication session is protected by utilizing transport encryption protocols such as TLS. TLS provides the Kubernetes API Server with a means to authenticate sessions and encrypt traffic.\n\nTo enable encrypted communication for Kubelet, the clientCAFile must be set. This parameter gives the location of the SSL Certificate Authority file used to secure Kubelet communication.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001184"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "90ad120b-82a2-509b-8850-9d105af8d205",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: Kubernetes Kubelet must have the SSL Certificate Authority set. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: Kubernetes Kubelet must have the SSL Certificate Authority set. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
