        {
          "group_id_src": "V-245544",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-245544",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000439-CTR-001080",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-245544",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000439-CTR-001080",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-245544r961632_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-245544r961632",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-002640",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes endpoints must use approved organizational certificate and key pair to protect information in transit.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes API Server manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Set the value of \"--kubelet-client-certificate\" and \"--kubelet-client-key\" to an Approved Organizational Certificate and key pair.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\ngrep -i kubelet-client-certificate *\ngrep -I kubelet-client-key * \n\nIf the setting \"--kubelet-client-certificate\" is not configured in the Kubernetes API server manifest file or contains no value, this is a finding.\n\nIf the setting \"--kubelet-client-key\" is not configured in the Kubernetes API server manifest file or contains no value, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Kubernetes control plane and external communication is managed by API Server. The main implementation of the API Server is to manage hardware resources for pods and container using horizontal or vertical scaling. Anyone who can gain access to the API Server can effectively control your Kubernetes architecture. Using authenticity protection, the communication can be protected against man-in-the-middle attacks/session hijacking and the insertion of false information into sessions.\n\nThe communication session is protected by utilizing transport encryption protocols, such as TLS. TLS provides the Kubernetes API Server with a means to be able to authenticate sessions and encrypt traffic.\n\nBy default, the API Server does not authenticate to the kubelet HTTPs endpoint. To enable secure communication for API Server, the parameter -kubelet-client-certificate and kubelet-client-key must be set. This parameter gives the location of the certificate and key pair used to secure API Server communication.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002448"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "504a7514-be16-5aba-9922-dab06a5e14a4",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: STRICT mesh mutual authentication and the gateway's TLS Secret references provide certificate/key-based protection for workload endpoints. Host/API endpoints remain inherited from MPN.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. STRICT mesh mutual authentication and the gateway's TLS Secret references provide certificate/key-based protection for workload endpoints. Host/API endpoints remain inherited from MPN.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-254800",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-254800",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000342-CTR-000775",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-254800",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000342-CTR-000775",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-254800r961359_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-254800r961359",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-002011",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes must have a Pod Security Admission control file configured.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes API Server manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. \n\nSet the value of \"--admission-control-config-file\" to a valid path for the file.\n\nCreate an admission controller config file:\nExample File:\n```yaml\napiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\nkind: AdmissionConfiguration\nplugins:\n- name: PodSecurity\n  configuration:\n    apiVersion: pod-security.admission.config.k8s.io/v1beta1\n    kind: PodSecurityConfiguration\n    # Defaults applied when a mode label is not set.\n    defaults:\n      enforce: \"privileged\"\n      enforce-version: \"latest\"\n    exemptions:\n      # Don't forget to exempt namespaces or users that are responsible for deploying\n      # cluster components, because they need to run privileged containers\n      usernames: [\"admin\"] \n      namespaces: [\"kube-system\"]\n\nSee for more details:\nMigrate from PSP to PSA:\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/migrate-from-psp/\n\nBest Practice: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-policy/#recommended-practice.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Control Plane. Run the command:\n\n\"grep -i admission-control-config-file *\"\n\nIf the setting \"--admission-control-config-file\" is not configured in the Kubernetes API Server manifest file, this is a finding.\n\nInspect the .yaml file defined by the --admission-control-config-file. Verify PodSecurity is properly configured. \nIf least privilege is not represented, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "An admission controller intercepts and processes requests to the Kubernetes API prior to persistence of the object, but after the request is authenticated and authorized.\n\nKubernetes (> v1.23)offers a built-in Pod Security admission controller to enforce the Pod Security Standards. Pod security restrictions are applied at the namespace level when pods are created. \n\nThe Kubernetes Pod Security Standards define different isolation levels for Pods. These standards define how to restrict the behavior of pods in a clear, consistent fashion.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002263"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "c46a8b44-eb89-59a3-91df-d6308bd6ed6f",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The least-privilege admission objective is implemented by Core Pepr, rather than an application-local or native PodSecurity configuration file: it mutates/denies unsafe workload security contexts and fails closed for ordinary mission namespaces. Treat this as a justified platform-equivalent implementation recommendation, not an unknown implementation and not evidence that --admission-control-config-file exists. The literal native-file check remains an assessor tailoring/inheritance item; Pepr exclusions and static host paths must stay in the platform boundary.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Attach the Pepr policy/exemption mapping as the alternate implementation and retain native-file tailoring in the MPN host checklist; do not add an application change merely to duplicate the platform control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-kubernetes-policy-code] re-kubernetes-policy-code\nUDS Pepr mutates and validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation, restricted capabilities, seccomp/proc/SELinux settings for pod/main/init/ephemeral security contexts. Workload policy admission supplies actual least-privilege enforcement independently of a native PodSecurity configuration file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[re-kubernetes-policy-reference] re-kubernetes-policy-reference\nUDS documents Pepr admission as aligned with Pod Security Standards restricted profile plus mesh controls. It also lists specific not-yet-implemented Big Bang policies, so this is evidence of the implemented policy set, not an assertion that Pepr exactly equals every PSS/native-controller behavior.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/reference/operator-and-crds/policy-engine.mdx\n\n[re-kubernetes-fail-closed] re-kubernetes-fail-closed\nUDS Pepr onError=reject; zarf admission/watch namespace is excluded, and istio-system has an additional Ignore-failure webhook for bootstrap availability. Ordinary mission namespaces do not share that fail-open exception.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/package.json\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The least-privilege admission objective is implemented by Core Pepr, rather than an application-local or native PodSecurity configuration file: it mutates/denies unsafe workload security contexts and fails closed for ordinary mission namespaces. Treat this as a justified platform-equivalent implementation recommendation, not an unknown implementation and not evidence that --admission-control-config-file exists. The literal native-file check remains an assessor tailoring/inheritance item; Pepr exclusions and static host paths must stay in the platform boundary.\n\nNext action: Attach the Pepr policy/exemption mapping as the alternate implementation and retain native-file tailoring in the MPN host checklist; do not add an application change merely to duplicate the platform control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-254801",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-254801",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000342-CTR-000775",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-254801",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000342-CTR-000775",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-254801r961359_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-254801r961359",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-002001",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes must enable PodSecurity admission controller on static pods and Kubelets.",
          "fix_text": "On the Control Plane, change to the manifests' directory at /etc/kubernetes/manifests and run the command:\ngrep -i feature-gates *\n\nEnsure the argument \"--feature-gates=PodSecurity=true\" is present in each manifest file.\n\nOn each Control Plane and Worker Node, run the command:\nps -ef | grep kubelet\n\nRemove the \"--feature-gates\" option if present.\n\nNote the path to the config file (identified by --config).\n\nEdit the Kubernetes Kubelet config file: \nAdd a \"featureGates\" setting if one does not yet exist. Add the feature gate \"PodSecurity=true\".\n\nRestart the kubelet service using the following command:\nsystemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart kubelet",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "On the Control Plane, change to the manifests' directory at /etc/kubernetes/manifests and run the command:\ngrep -i feature-gates *\n\nFor each manifest file, if the \"--feature-gates\" setting does not exist, does not contain the \"--PodSecurity\" flag, or sets the flag to \"false\", this is a finding.\n\nOn each Control Plane and Worker Node, run the command:\nps -ef | grep kubelet\n\nIf the \"--feature-gates\" option exists, this is a finding. \n\nNote the path to the config file (identified by --config).\n\nInspect the content of the config file:\nIf the \"featureGates\" setting is not present, does not contain the \"PodSecurity\" flag, or sets the flag to \"false\", this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "PodSecurity admission controller is a component that validates and enforces security policies for pods running within a Kubernetes cluster. It is responsible for evaluating the security context and configuration of pods against defined policies. \n\nTo enable PodSecurity admission controller on Static Pods (kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager, or kube-schedule), the argument \"--feature-gates=PodSecurity=true\" must be set.\n\nTo enable PodSecurity admission controller on Kubelets, the featureGates PodSecurity=true argument must be set.\n\n(Note: The PodSecurity feature gate is GA as of  v1.25.)",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
