          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core provides workload admission enforcement through Pepr, while supported modern Kubernetes has native PodSecurity functionality. The literal PodSecurity=true feature-gate prescription is obsolete on1.28+ because the feature gate was removed after GA. The correct recommendation is version-aware platform inheritance/tailoring, not adding a removed flag or reporting missing application code. Pepr does not itself govern kubelet static-pod startup; that part remains within the MPN host-control mapping, and actual MPN version is not inferred from Core test versions.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Document the actual host Kubernetes version in the inheritance package and tailor obsolete feature-gate text; map static-pod/kubelet restrictions to MPN host controls and normal workload admission to Pepr.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-kubernetes-policy-code] re-kubernetes-policy-code\nUDS Pepr mutates and validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation, restricted capabilities, seccomp/proc/SELinux settings for pod/main/init/ephemeral security contexts. Workload policy admission supplies actual least-privilege enforcement independently of a native PodSecurity configuration file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[re-kubernetes-fail-closed] re-kubernetes-fail-closed\nUDS Pepr onError=reject; zarf admission/watch namespace is excluded, and istio-system has an additional Ignore-failure webhook for bootstrap availability. Ordinary mission namespaces do not share that fail-open exception.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/package.json\n\n[re-kubernetes-removed-gate] re-kubernetes-removed-gate\nUpstream Kubernetes lists PodSecurity feature gate stable/default true from1.25 through1.27; it is removed after1.27. A removed feature gate is not a valid setting, so requiring PodSecurity=true literally on1.28+ is obsolete, not a remediation action.\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/command-line-tools-reference/feature-gates-removed/\n\n[re-kubernetes-native-admission] re-kubernetes-native-admission\nPodSecurity is the built-in validating admission controller stable since1.25 and among default-enabled admission plugins. Native enablement can still be explicitly overridden; source of a separate app package does not prove the exact host override or static-pod configuration.\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers/\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core provides workload admission enforcement through Pepr, while supported modern Kubernetes has native PodSecurity functionality. The literal PodSecurity=true feature-gate prescription is obsolete on1.28+ because the feature gate was removed after GA. The correct recommendation is version-aware platform inheritance/tailoring, not adding a removed flag or reporting missing application code. Pepr does not itself govern kubelet static-pod startup; that part remains within the MPN host-control mapping, and actual MPN version is not inferred from Core test versions.\n\nNext action: Document the actual host Kubernetes version in the inheritance package and tailor obsolete feature-gate text; map static-pod/kubelet restrictions to MPN host controls and normal workload admission to Pepr."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-274882",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-274882",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000100",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-274882",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000033-CTR-000100",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-274882r1137640_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-274882r1137640",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001162",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes Secrets must be encrypted at rest.",
          "fix_text": "Edit the Kubernetes API Server manifest file in the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Master Node. \n\nSet the value of \"--encryption-provider-config\" to the path to the encryption config. \n\nThe encryption config must specify the Secret's resource and provider. Below is an example:\n{\n  \"kind\": \"EncryptionConfiguration\",\n  \"apiVersion\": \"apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\",\n  \"resources\": [\n    {\n      \"resources\": [\n        \"secrets\"\n      ],\n      \"providers\": [\n        {\n          \"aescbc\": {\n            \"keys\": [\n              {\n                \"name\": \"aescbckey\",\n                \"secret\": \"xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"identity\": {}\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Change to the /etc/kubernetes/manifests directory on the Kubernetes Master Node. Run the command:\ngrep -i encryption-provider-config *\n\nIf the setting \"encryption-provider-config\" is not configured, this is a finding.\n\nIf the setting is configured, check the contents of the file specified by its argument. \n\nIf the file does not specify the Secret's resource, this is a finding. \n\nIf the identity provider is specified as the first provider for the resource, this is also a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Kubernetes Secrets may store sensitive information such as passwords, tokens, and keys. These values are stored in the etcd database used by Kubernetes unencrypted. To protect these Secrets at rest, these values must be encrypted.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000213"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "af0c7fba-b928-5e38-818d-097805d1c3bf",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: Kubernetes Secrets must be encrypted at rest. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-supported-versions] re-kubernetes-supported-versions\nPinned Core1.14 documentation covers supported non-EOL CNCF-conformant clusters and tests1.34/1.35/1.36 variants. This supports modern-version tailoring; it does not identify the actual MPN host version.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/supported-distributions.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Inherited MPN host-control recommendation for: Kubernetes Secrets must be encrypted at rest. The official check addresses host/control-plane binaries, flags, files, native logging, node services or encryption, not an application behavior. The selected Horizon package deploys namespaced workloads atop Core and does not provision those host settings. Current scope assigns that boundary to MPN; this designation means proposed inherited coverage, not source confirmation of the exact host setting or a formal pass. No application remediation is inferred from absence of the host configuration in the application repo.\n\nNext action: Carry the exact official check into the MPN-owned Kubernetes baseline/checklist and link its inherited result; do not treat the ownership assertion as evidence of an observed native setting."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-274883",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-274883",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000171-CTR-000435",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-274883",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000171-CTR-000435",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-274883r1107239_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-274883r1107239",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001161",
          "rule_title": "Sensitive information must be stored using Kubernetes Secrets or an external Secret store provider.",
          "fix_text": "Any sensitive information found must be stored in an approved external Secret store provider or use Kubernetes Secrets (attached on an as-needed basis to pods).",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "On the Kubernetes Master node, run the following command:\nkubectl get all,cm -A -o yaml \n\nManually review the output for sensitive information.\n\nIf any sensitive information is found, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Sensitive information, such as passwords, keys, and tokens must not be stored in application code.\n\nKubernetes offers a resource called Secrets that are designed for storing sensitive information for use by applications. Secrets are created and managed separately from application code. Additionally, they can be encrypted at rest and access to the secrets can be controlled via RBAC.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004062"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "3cb54fb6-ed43-55f9-9f2f-ff92d2071d44",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual pushed UDS profile retains SeaweedFS credential-bearing s3.json in a ConfigMap, mounted read-only into the service. S3 authentication is already implemented. The remaining gap is Kubernetes storage/access classification: ConfigMap read permission is broader than Secret read permission and etcd Secret encryption does not transform this ConfigMap into a Secret. Mesh, non-root execution and read-only mounts do not supply the missing secret-store boundary.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Store the S3 configuration in a protected Secret/external store and mount it as needed, or document an accepted compensating-control exception. No new S3 authentication implementation is required.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-s3-configmap] re-kubernetes-s3-configmap\nSeaweedFS s3.json contains accessKey/secretKey in a Kubernetes ConfigMap. Deployment mounts that ConfigMap read-only. Re-rendering actual UDS identity/runtime-image profile preserves this resource kind and sensitive fields; the eventual Zarf substitutions populate credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/seaweedfs/templates/configmap.yaml\n\n[re-kubernetes-profile-transform] re-kubernetes-profile-transform\nUDS identity transformation removes the bundled Keycloak component/secrets and edits OIDC/profile values, but does not replace the PostgreSQL secret-as-environment template or SeaweedFS credential ConfigMap. runtime_source.stage pins image fields and leaves those templates unchanged.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/platform_identity.py",
          "finding_details": "Actual pushed UDS profile retains SeaweedFS credential-bearing s3.json in a ConfigMap, mounted read-only into the service. S3 authentication is already implemented. The remaining gap is Kubernetes storage/access classification: ConfigMap read permission is broader than Secret read permission and etcd Secret encryption does not transform this ConfigMap into a Secret. Mesh, non-root execution and read-only mounts do not supply the missing secret-store boundary.\n\nNext action: Store the S3 configuration in a protected Secret/external store and mount it as needed, or document an accepted compensating-control exception. No new S3 authentication implementation is required."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-274884",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-274884",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000429-CTR-001060",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-274884",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000429-CTR-001060",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-274884r1107245_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-274884r1107245",
          "rule_version": "CNTR-K8-001163",
          "rule_title": "Kubernetes must limit Secret access on a need-to-know basis.",
          "fix_text": "For Kubernetes accounts that have read access to Secrets without a documented requirement, modify the corresponding Role or ClusterRole to remove list, watch, and get privileges for Secrets.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the Kubernetes accounts and their corresponding roles. \n\nIf any accounts have read (list, watch, get) access to Secrets without a documented organizational requirement, this is a finding. \n\nRun the below command to list the workload resources for applications deployed to Kubernetes:\nkubectl get all -A -o yaml \n\nIf Secrets are attached to applications without a documented requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Kubernetes_STIG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Kubernetes secrets may store sensitive information such as passwords, tokens, and keys. Access to these secrets should be limited to a need-to-know basis via Kubernetes RBAC.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002476"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5376",
          "uuid": "f614c141-99d3-5a0f-99c7-dbc0559d3891",
          "stig_uuid": "92b6f33f-228f-5363-afb6-9f95dc57a4b5",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Candidate workloads use dedicated service identities with service-account-token automount disabled where declared, and no application Role/ClusterRole granting Secret reads was found in the reviewed app charts. This supports restricted workload access. The exact effective API Secret permissions still belong to MPN/UDS RBAC inheritance; namespace network default-deny alone does not authorize or restrict Kubernetes Secret reads. Keep this as a conditional platform/workload coverage recommendation rather than claiming network policy proves the rule.\n\nComponents: MPN cluster and nodes; Gallatin horizon-uds workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (UDS Core); MPN node/control-plane owner for host configuration\n\nNext action: Retain the selected service-account manifests and MPN Secret RBAC inheritance; document necessary operator/provisioner read grants and ensure ordinary users lack broad Secret-list/read permissions.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-kubernetes-release] re-kubernetes-release\nLatest successful Nov5 UDS push artifact2026.45.4-uds.1 identifies package commit d528b7f, Horizon b84b9af, Core1.14 and identity.mode=uds. Its build-info supplies immutable runtimeSource images; this supersedes the bundled-identity default candidate for this review.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-kubernetes-workload-sa] re-kubernetes-workload-sa\nApplication chart creates named service accounts and explicitly controls automountServiceAccountToken. That supports workload isolation but does not establish the separate controller-manager --use-service-account-credentials flag.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/serviceaccount.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Candidate workloads use dedicated service identities with service-account-token automount disabled where declared, and no application Role/ClusterRole granting Secret reads was found in the reviewed app charts. This supports restricted workload access. The exact effective API Secret permissions still belong to MPN/UDS RBAC inheritance; namespace network default-deny alone does not authorize or restrict Kubernetes Secret reads. Keep this as a conditional platform/workload coverage recommendation rather than claiming network policy proves the rule.\n\nNext action: Retain the selected service-account manifests and MPN Secret RBAC inheritance; document necessary operator/provisioner read grants and ensure ordinary users lack broad Secret-list/read permissions."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
